---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const installSnippet = `npm install -D @kernelui-lib/cli
# or run once without installing:
npx @kernelui-lib/cli init`;

const initSnippet = `kernel init
kernel init --tailwind --accent blue
kernel init --dry-run`;

const doctorSnippet = `kernel doctor
kernel doctor --path ./apps/web`;

const docsSnippet = `kernel docs
kernel docs text-field --json
kernel docs dialog --markdown`;

const agentsSnippet = `kernel agents                    # skills + MCP (.mcp.json) + AGENTS.md
kernel agents --client cursor    # register MCP for Cursor instead
kernel agents --dir .agents/skills --no-mcp`;

const mcpSnippet = `kernel mcp init                  # Claude Code: writes .mcp.json
kernel mcp init --client cursor  # .cursor/mcp.json
kernel mcp init --client vscode  # .vscode/mcp.json
kernel mcp init --client codex   # .codex/config.toml`;

const mcpManualSnippet = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "kernel": { "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@kernelui-lib/cli", "mcp"] }
  }
}`;

const checkSnippet = `kernel check src/App.tsx src/Settings.tsx`;
---

<BaseLayout title="CLI" description="Use the Kernel CLI to install packages, wire up styles, diagnose setup, and look up component metadata locally." withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>CLI</h1>
    <p>
      The Kernel CLI handles the mechanical parts of integrating the library:
      installing packages, adding the required stylesheet imports, and checking
      that your project is wired up correctly. It does <em>not</em> copy
      component source into your repo the way shadcn/ui does — Kernel ships as
      npm packages.
    </p>

    <h2>Install</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={installSnippet} lang="bash" />

    <h2><code>kernel init</code></h2>
    <p>
      Detects your package manager and framework (Vite or Next.js), installs
      <code>@kernelui-lib/react</code> and <code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code>,
      then idempotently adds the required CSS imports.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={initSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>Options:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>--tailwind</code> — use the Tailwind v4 bridge instead of plain CSS imports</li>
      <li><code>--accent &lt;name&gt;</code> — import a named accent theme (amber, blue, green, orange, red, teal, violet)</li>
      <li><code>--tokens-only</code> — install only <code>@kernelui-lib/styles</code></li>
      <li><code>--dry-run</code> — preview file changes without writing</li>
      <li><code>--package-manager npm|yarn|pnpm|bun</code> — override lockfile detection</li>
      <li><code>--framework vite|next</code> — override framework detection</li>
    </ul>

    <h2><code>kernel doctor</code></h2>
    <p>
      Verifies React 18+, package installation, and stylesheet imports. If
      <code>components.json</code> or <code>@radix-ui/*</code> packages are
      present, it points you to the <a href="/migration/">migration guide</a>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={doctorSnippet} lang="bash" />

    <h2><code>kernel docs</code></h2>
    <p>
      Looks up component metadata from the local registry — useful for agents and
      offline development. Accepts a slug, component name, or shadcn alias.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={docsSnippet} lang="bash" />

    <h2><code>kernel agents</code></h2>
    <p>
      Makes a project agent-ready in one step. It installs Kernel's agent
      skills into <code>.claude/skills</code>, registers the MCP server
      below, and adds a short Kernel UI section to <code>AGENTS.md</code>
      (and <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, if the project has one). It's safe to run
      again: it replaces its own section rather than adding another.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={agentsSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>
      The skills ship inside <code>@kernelui-lib/react</code> and
      <code>@kernelui-lib/elements</code> (in their <code>skills/</code>
      folders), so they always match the version you installed:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>kernel-ui</code>: the workflow and conventions for building React UI with Kernel.</li>
      <li><code>kernel-ui-theming</code>: accent, radius, spacing, dark mode and icon-set tokens.</li>
      <li><code>kernel-ui-migrate</code>: moving from shadcn/ui or Radix.</li>
      <li><code>kernel-ui-elements</code>: building with the Custom Elements.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2><code>kernel mcp</code></h2>
    <p>
      Runs a Model Context Protocol server, so a coding agent can look Kernel
      up instead of guessing. It answers from data bundled into the CLI,
      so it works offline and always matches the version you installed.
      Register it with your agent once:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={mcpSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>Or add it by hand to any MCP client:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={mcpManualSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <p>Its tools:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>list_components</code> and <code>search_components</code>: what exists, and the right component for a need described in plain words.</li>
      <li><code>get_component</code>: exact React props (types, defaults, notes), custom element attributes and events, why the component is built on its native element, and notes for migrating from shadcn or Radix.</li>
      <li><code>get_tokens</code>: design tokens with their values and what each is for.</li>
      <li><code>get_conventions</code>: install steps and the conventions every component shares.</li>
      <li><code>check_code</code>: reviews code the agent just wrote (see <code>kernel check</code> below).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2><code>kernel check</code></h2>
    <p>
      Reviews files for the mistakes that come up most when people and agents
      write Kernel code, with the fix spelled out: props from other libraries
      (<code>asChild</code>, <code>leftIcon</code>), invalid values
      (<code>variant="primry"</code>), fields with no <code>label</code>,
      icon-only buttons with no <code>aria-label</code>, clickable
      <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>s, raw colours, hand-picked pill radii, and
      shadcn or Radix imports Kernel replaces. It exits with an error when it
      finds one, so it can run in CI.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={checkSnippet} lang="bash" />

    <h2>LLM assets</h2>
    <p>
      Kernel publishes several machine-readable surfaces:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>llms.txt</code> in <code>@kernelui-lib/react</code> and <code>@kernelui-lib/elements</code> (shipped with npm)</li>
      <li><a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a> and <a href="/llms-full.txt">/llms-full.txt</a> on the docs site</li>
      <li><a href="/registry.json">/registry.json</a> — structured component catalog with shadcn aliases and migration caveats</li>
      <li><a href="/api.json">/api.json</a> — every component's exact props, attributes and events, generated from source</li>
      <li><code>kernel docs &lt;component&gt; --json</code> for local lookup, and <code>kernel mcp</code> for agents</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Exact prop types always live in <code>dist/index.d.ts</code> inside each
      package. The registry and <code>llms.txt</code> files are maps, not the
      source of truth for types.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
